@page
@{
	ViewData["Title"] = "Dakar 1985";
	Layout = "~/Pages/Shared/_Layout.cshtml";
}
<div class="text-center">
	<h1>
		Summary (1985 - Paris - Algiers - Dakar)
	</h1>
	<p>
		<img src="~/images/dakar/review/1985/1985_map.jpg" style="float: left; height: 150px" />The
		seventh Paris - Algiers -
		<img src="~/images/dakar/review/1985/1985_02.jpg" style="float: right; height: 118px" />Dakar,
		which took place from 1 - 22 January, was at 14,000 km the toughest ever planned,
		with a starting field of 503 entrants, of whom 360 competed in the car/truck
		category. This time the Pajero was even more popular, with three vehicles
		under Sonauto management and no fewer than 37 entries from private teams. Cowan was
		once again the team leader alongside Patrick Zaniroli in the second machine, this
		time starting in the prototype class. From the majestic palace of Versailles,
		near Paris, the Mitsubishi team, led by Cowan and
		his capable navigator Johnstone Syers, set out towards the hostile climate of the
		Sahara, an arena with little sympathy for the weak and unreliable,
		where the greatest battle in the history of motorsport was to be fought.
		With temperatures above 40°C and the merciless pounding of the stony desert,
		through which hundreds of cars race at top speed, the Dakar is the ultimate proving grou<img src="~/images/dakar/review/1985/1985_01.jpg" style="float: right; height: 110px" />nd
		for any motor-driven vehicle. For the Pajero, the third year of participation
		was simply another chapter in the Pajero's rise as the toughest
		high-performance off-road machine in the world. Under the guidance of Sonauto Mitsubishi chief
		Ulrich Brehmer, Cowan and Zaniroli drove towards their destiny at the Dakar,
		while the popular Japanese actor Yohsuke Natsuki had to learn what it
		means to be away from the stage and to tackle the ultimate competition in a standard
		Pajero. In the Tenere desert, the Mitsubishi Pajero took the
		spotlight as Zaniroli took the lead, closely followed by Cowan. Where all
		the others failed, the Pajero ran in a class of its own. After 22 days, the
		survivors reached the fresh, cooling breeze of the Atlantic in Dakar, and Zaniroli
		and Cowan celebrated a magnificent one-two victory, and that after only three years.
		There was no longer any doubt - the Pajero was the King of the Desert.
	</p>
